 "summary": "## gyrA (Rv0006) \u2014 DNA gyrase subunit A\n\n**Identity.** KEGG resolved `gyrA` to **mtu:Rv0006** by symbol. UniProt reviewed entry **P9WG47** (GYRA_MYCTU, locus tag Rv0006); two further unreviewed entries matched the symbol, **M4PN16** and **F2X2D0**, both with no FUNCTION statement (per the tool notes, \"the entry exists but its function is not described\").\n\n**Function (experimental, UniProt P9WG47).** The reviewed entry carries an experimentally supported FUNCTION statement: \"A type II topoisomerase that negatively supercoils closed circular double-stranded (ds) DNA in an ATP-dependent manner to maintain chromosomes in an underwound state, while in the absence of ATP it relaxes supercoiled dsDNA\", and it \"Also catalyzes the interconversion of other topological isomers of dsDNA rings, including catenanes\". A mycobacterium-specific point is explicit in the same record: \"Gyrase from M.tuberculosis has higher decatenation than supercoiling activity compared to E.coli; as M.tuberculosis only has 1 type II topoisomerase, gyrase has to fulfill the decatenation function of topoisomerase IV as well\" \u2014 i.e. this single enzyme covers both the supercoiling and the chromosome-decatenation duties that are split between two enzymes in E. coli. Quantitatively it is the weaker enzyme: \"At comparable concentrations M.tuberculosis gyrase cannot introduce as many negative supercoils into DNA as the E.coli enzyme, and its ATPase activity is lower\". Catalytic activity is listed as \"ATP-dependent breakage, passage and rejoining of double-stranded DNA.\" UniProt cites PMIDs 15047530, 16377674, 16876125, 17015625, 18426901, 19060136, 20805881, 22457352, 22844097 and 23869946 as the experimental support (that is UniProt's curated evidence, not literature I retrieved here).\n\n**Complex.** Experimentally supported subunit statement in P9WG47: \"Heterotetramer, composed of two GyrA and two GyrB chains. In the heterotetramer, GyrA contains the active site tyrosine that forms a transient covalent intermediate with DNA, while GyrB binds cofactors and catalyzes ATP hydrolysis\". The entry is extensively structurally characterised \u2014 19 PDB entries, including 3IFZ, 5BS8, 6GAU and 9FOY.\n\n**Pathways.** KEGG is the silent source here, not biology: `mtu:Rv0006` exists in KEGG but \"is not mapped to any pathway in this organism.\" UniProt supplies the functional placement instead (type II topoisomerase activity on the chromosome). Note that the second, untiered UniProt statement linking supercoiling to \"DNA replication, transcription, recombination and repair\" carries no evidence code and should be read as generic topoisomerase background, not a measurement on this protein.\n\n**Notable interaction partners (STRING, species 83332, score \u2265700).**\n- **83332.Rv0005 (gyrB)** \u2014 combined 0.999, and the only partner with strong non-textmining support in every relevant channel (experimental 0.981, coexpression 0.941, neighbourhood 0.830, cooccurrence 0.773). This is the genuine physical partner and matches the UniProt heterotetramer statement.\n- **83332.Rv0007** \u2014 combined 0.946, driven by genomic neighbourhood (0.774) and coexpression (0.757) with negligible textmining (0.094); an operonic/context-based association, not a literature artefact.\n- **83332.Rv0002 (dnaN)** 0.887 and **83332.Rv0003 (recF)** 0.861 \u2014 both mainly neighbourhood (0.604, 0.524), consistent with the conserved *gyrB\u2013gyrA\u2013recF\u2013dnaN* chromosomal region rather than demonstrated complex formation.\n- Transcription/translation machinery links \u2014 **83332.Rv0667 (rpoB)** 0.980, **83332.Rv3457c (rpoA)** 0.941, **83332.Rv0685 (tuf)** 0.873, **83332.Rv0684 (fusA1)** 0.859 \u2014 pass the non-textmining threshold only on coexpression (0.455, 0.536, 0.463, 0.448); their scores are dominated by textmining.\n- **Drug-resistance co-mention cluster, textmining only:** 83332.Rv1484 (inhA), 83332.Rv2043c (pncA), 83332.Rv3795 (embB), 83332.Rv3794 (embA), 83332.Rv0682 (rpsL), 83332.Rv0668 (rpoC), 83332.Rv1694 (tlyA), 83332.Rv3919c (gid), 83332.Rv1908c (katG), 83332.Rv2737c (recA), 83332.Rv2150c (ftsZ), 83332.Rv2416c (eis) all have `evidence_beyond_textmining: false`. These are co-mentions in resistance-genotyping papers \u2014 one line of evidence, literature \u2014 and should not be read as functional or physical interactions.\n\n**Lineage marker.** `lineage_markers` returned no records: \"No lineage-defining position falls within gyrA in the tbdb barcode.\" The tool notes this as an informative negative (855 of 4,008 H37Rv genes carry one). So gyrA variants are not, by this barcode, confounded by lineage structure at the gene level \u2014 though any specific association should still be checked against genotype data, which this lookup cannot do.\n\n**Drug-resistance catalogue.** gyrA **is resistance-associated** (WHO catalogue v2 via tbdb), for **levofloxacin and moxifloxacin**. Of 1,607 catalogued variants: 18 \"Assoc w R\", 2 \"Assoc w R - Interim\", 23 \"Not assoc w R\", 721 \"Not assoc w R - Interim\", 833 \"Uncertain significance\", 10 ungraded. The associated variants cluster in the quinolone-resistance region: `tbdb:gyrA:p.Gly88Cys`, `tbdb:gyrA:p.Gly88Ala` (interim), `tbdb:gyrA:p.Asp89Asn`, `tbdb:gyrA:p.Ala90Val`, `tbdb:gyrA:p.Ser91Pro`, and `tbdb:gyrA:p.Asp94Ala`/`Asn`/`Gly`/`His`/`Tyr`, with moxifloxacin records distinguishing high-level (e.g. `tbdb:gyrA:p.Asp94Gly`, comment \"High-level resistance\") from low-level (e.g. `tbdb:gyrA:p.Ala90Val`). This flag is a property of the gene: most gyrA variants are graded uncertain or not associated, so it is not evidence that any given gyrA mutation confers resistance.\n\n**Bottom line.** gyrA encodes the DNA-cleaving subunit of the sole M. tuberculosis type II topoisomerase, acting as a GyrA\u2082GyrB\u2082 heterotetramer that supercoils and decatenates the chromosome; its one well-supported protein partner is GyrB, its other high-scoring STRING links are genomic-context (Rv0007, dnaN, recF) or literature co-mention; KEGG has no pathway for it while UniProt is rich; and it carries no lineage-defining barcode position.",
